# Frontend application structure

Use this layout inside each Angular app (`shell`, `*-mfe`):

```
src/app/
├── core/                 # App-wide infrastructure (singleton)
│   ├── auth/
│   ├── http/
│   ├── services/
│   ├── config/
│   └── core.providers.ts
├── shared/               # Reusable UI / utilities (no feature domain)
│   ├── components/
│   ├── directives/
│   ├── pipes/
│   ├── validators/
│   └── shared.models.ts
├── layout/               # Shell chrome (header / sidebar / footer)
├── features/             # Business capabilities
│   └── {feature}/
│       ├── {use-case}/           # e.g. user-list/, create-user/
│       │   ├── {use-case}.page.ts
│       │   ├── {use-case}.html
│       │   ├── {use-case}.scss
│       │   └── {use-case}.store.ts   # optional page store
│       ├── {feature}.api.ts
│       ├── {feature}.models.ts
│       ├── {feature}.store.ts        # optional feature store (signals)
│       └── {feature}.routes.ts
├── app.routes.ts
├── app.config.ts
└── app.component.ts
```

## Rules

1. **Features own screens** — one folder per use case under `features/{feature}/`; no `data-access/` or `pages/` buckets.
2. **API + models** live at the feature root (`users.api.ts`, `users.models.ts`).
3. **Signals stores** — prefer feature-level `{feature}.store.ts` when shared across screens; page-level `{use-case}.store.ts` when local.
4. **Pages** use separate `{name}.page.ts` + `{name}.html` + `{name}.scss` (no inline templates).
5. **Presentational widgets** used by one feature stay under that feature (or `shared/components` if reused cross-feature).
6. **`core/`** — auth, HTTP interceptors, app config providers only; do not put business screens here.
7. **`layout/`** — application chrome; MFEs may omit layout when the shell already owns it.
8. Remotes still expose `remote.routes.ts` (or `app.routes.ts`) that compose feature route arrays.
9. Call gateway `/api/...` only — never downstream ports.
10. Shared cross-MFE library remains `projects/shared` (LanguageStore, SessionApi, etc.); app `shared/` is local to that app.
